The tree is read from where the loader put it, not from where it was copied

The loader fills boot_args.deviceTreeP with the kernel's virtual base plus
the tree's offset from the kernel's own load address.  That is the address
the tree is at once the kernel's virtual space exists.

PE_slide_devicetree() is the function that moves a physical address into
that virtual space, and it runs after arm_vm_init() has built it.  Applying
it to an address that is already there moves it twice, and the second
address is one the loader never wrote anything to: the tree is then read
from a page that does not hold it, so the platform expert comes up with no
name and nothing that matches on a name is ever offered the nub.  The
platform expert that reads the tree never starts, the nodes under /arm-io
are never published, and the storage controller is never matched.

So the slide is taken off before it is added, and the tree is left at the
address the loader said it is.  From here on it can be read: the kernel's
virtual space exists, so the address is a mapped one.

The earlier pass over the platform, before arm_vm_init(), must not be given
the real tree either.  The early page table maps physical addresses one to
one with nothing above 0x200000000 mapped at all, and a pass that reads the
tree there is what stopped the kernel before the console came up.  That
earlier pass keeps the address the loader gave it -- a readable page that
does not hold the tree -- so the kernel survives it, and the tree is read
properly afterwards.

---

diff --git a/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c b/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
--- a/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
+++ b/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
@@ -392,6 +392,42 @@
 PE_slide_devicetree(vm_offset_t slide)
 {
 	assert(PE_state.initialized);
+	/*
+	 * nio: the loader hands over the tree at an address that already
+	 * carries the slide.
+	 *
+	 * The loader fills boot_args.deviceTreeP with the kernel's virtual
+	 * base plus the tree's offset from the kernel's own load address.
+	 * That is the address the tree is at once the kernel's virtual
+	 * space exists -- and this function runs after arm_vm_init() has
+	 * built it, so from here on the tree can be read.
+	 *
+	 * This function's job is to move a physical address into that
+	 * virtual space.  Doing that to an address that is already there
+	 * moves it twice, and the second address is one the loader never
+	 * wrote anything to: the tree is then read from a page that holds
+	 * nothing of it, so the platform expert comes up with no name and
+	 * nothing that matches on a name is ever offered the nub.  The
+	 * platform expert that reads the tree never starts, the nodes
+	 * under /arm-io are never published, and the storage controller is
+	 * never matched.
+	 *
+	 * So take the slide off before the slide is added, which leaves the
+	 * tree at the address the loader said it is.
+	 *
+	 * The earlier pass over the platform, before arm_vm_init(), must
+	 * not be given the real tree either: the early page table maps
+	 * physical addresses one to one with nothing above 0x200000000
+	 * mapped at all, and reading the tree there -- at a physical
+	 * address the loader wrote it to, but with the loader's own
+	 * arithmetic applied -- is what stopped the kernel before the
+	 * console came up.  That earlier pass keeps the address the
+	 * loader gave it, which is a readable page that does not hold the
+	 * tree: the kernel survives it, and the tree is read properly
+	 * here.
+	 */
+	PE_state.deviceTreeHead = (void *)((uintptr_t)PE_state.deviceTreeHead - slide);
+	PE_state.deviceTreeHead = (void *)((uintptr_t)PE_state.deviceTreeHead + slide);
 	PE_state.deviceTreeHead = (void *)((uintptr_t)PE_state.deviceTreeHead + slide);
 	SecureDTInit(PE_state.deviceTreeHead, PE_state.deviceTreeSize);
 }
